  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "The authors evaluated equitable access to four urban ecosystem services—recreation, cooling, habitat quality, and water yield—across Dhaka using InVEST models, a 15-minute walking accessibility metric, and fuzzy overlay mapping. Access varied dramatically by service type, with 51% of wards showing very low justice indices for cultural services and 47% for supporting services. Spatial modeling revealed a persistent north-south disparity, where Dhaka South consistently underperformed compared to Dhaka North, while high access remained clustered around specific areas like Hatirjheel.",
      "why": "This provides empirical baseline measurements for environmental justice in a rapidly growing Global South megacity, demonstrating that physical ecosystem service supply alone does not translate to equitable access when socio-spatial barriers exist."
    }
  },
